  learn: { title: 'Transform a Value with let, Work on a Receiver with run', subtitle: 'Both let and run execute a block and return the block’s final expression. The key difference is how you access the context object: let provides it as an argument, usually `it`, while run provides it as the receiver, `this`.', exampleTag: 'EXAMPLE', exampleTitle: 'Two ways to calculate from the same value', language: 'Kotlin', codeSnippet: ['fun main() {', '  val word = "kotlin"', '', '  val letResult = word.let {', '    it.uppercase()', '  }', '', '  val runResult = word.run {', '    uppercase() + "!"', '  }', '', '  println(letResult)', '  println(runResult)', '}'], explanation: 'word.let { ... } passes "kotlin" into the lambda as its argument, so `it.uppercase()` produces "KOTLIN". word.run { ... } makes "kotlin" the lambda receiver, so its members can be called directly; `uppercase() + "!"` produces "KOTLIN!". In both cases, the value of the lambda’s final expression becomes the result of the whole scope-function call.', keyIdeas: [{ number: 1, title: 'let uses a lambda argument', description: 'Inside let, the context object is available as `it` by default. You can also give that argument a descriptive name.' }, { number: 2, title: 'run uses a lambda receiver', description: 'Inside run, the context object is `this`, so its properties and functions can usually be accessed without writing `this.` explicitly.' }, { number: 3, title: 'Both return the lambda result', description: 'The final expression inside let or run becomes the value returned by the scope-function call.' }, { number: 4, title: 'Safe-call let runs only for a non-null receiver', description: 'A common pattern is nullableValue?.let { ... }. The block is skipped when nullableValue is null.' }], keyTakeaway: 'Use let when treating the context object as a lambda argument, and run when working naturally with its members as a receiver; both return the lambda’s final result.' },
  explore: { title: 'Explore the Concept', subtitle: 'Compare `it` and receiver-style access, then see how lambda results and nullable safe calls affect the final value.', cards: [
    { id: 'let-run-explore-1', number: '01', title: 'Transform a value with let', language: 'Kotlin', subtitle: 'let exposes the context object as `it` and returns the lambda result.', code: ['val name = "Mira"', 'val label = name.let {', '  "User: " + it', '}', 'println(label)'], whatItMeans: [{ label: 'it', description: 'Refers to the String stored in name.' }, { label: '"User: " + it', description: 'This is the final expression, so let returns "User: Mira".' }], whatChanged: 'Used let to transform one value into a different result.' },
    { id: 'let-run-explore-2', number: '02', title: 'Give the let argument a clear name', language: 'Kotlin', subtitle: 'The implicit `it` name is convenient, but a descriptive parameter can improve clarity.', code: ['val price = 250', 'val message = price.let { amount ->', '  "Total: " + amount', '}', 'println(message)'], whatItMeans: [{ label: 'amount ->', description: 'Renames the lambda argument from the implicit `it` to amount.' }, { label: 'amount', description: 'Still refers to the same context value, 250.' }], whatChanged: 'Replaced `it` with a descriptive lambda-parameter name without changing let semantics.' },
    { id: 'let-run-explore-3', number: '03', title: 'Work with members naturally using run', language: 'Kotlin', subtitle: 'run makes the object the receiver, so member access can be concise.', code: ['val text = "scope"', 'val result = text.run {', '  uppercase() + " has " + length + " letters"', '}', 'println(result)'], whatItMeans: [{ label: 'uppercase()', description: 'Calls a String function on the run receiver.' }, { label: 'length', description: 'Reads the receiver’s String length without writing this.length.' }, { label: 'Final expression', description: 'The built String becomes the result returned by run.' }], whatChanged: 'Used receiver-style access to call multiple members of the same object.' },
    { id: 'let-run-explore-4', number: '04', title: 'Safe-call let skips null', language: 'Kotlin', subtitle: 'Combining `?.` with let runs the block only when the nullable receiver is non-null.', code: ['val nickname: String? = null', 'val length = nickname?.let {', '  it.length', '}', 'println(length)'], whatItMeans: [{ label: 'nickname?.let', description: 'Because nickname is null, the let block does not execute.' }, { label: 'length', description: 'The whole safe-call expression evaluates to null.' }], whatChanged: 'Used let as part of a null-safe operation and observed that a null receiver skips the block.' },
    { id: 'let-run-explore-5', number: '05', title: 'The last expression controls the result', language: 'Kotlin', subtitle: 'Printing inside the block does not determine what let or run returns.', code: ['val number = 7', 'val result = number.run {', '  println("Working with " + this)', '  this * 3', '}', 'println(result)'], whatItMeans: [{ label: 'println(...)', description: 'Performs a side effect, but its value is not the final result of the block.' }, { label: 'this * 3', description: 'This is the final expression, so run returns 21.' }], whatChanged: 'Separated an intermediate side effect from the lambda’s actual return value.' },
    { id: "world-13-let-run-explore-6", number: "06", title: "Nullable let", language: 'Kotlin', subtitle: "A null receiver's safe-call let never runs its block.", code: ["val name:String?=null","name?.let {","  println(it.uppercase())","}","println(\"done\")"], whatItMeans: [{ label: 'name?.let', description: "Skipped entirely because name is null -- the block inside never executes." }], whatChanged: "Combined the safe-call operator with let so the whole block is skipped for a null receiver." }] },
